\\boxed{12}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to prove that it's possible to arrange 8 points on a plane such that there are at least 12 \"effective circles,\" where an \"effective circle\" is defined as one that passes through exactly 4 of these points.\n\n1. **Form a Triangle**: \n   Begin by constructing a triangle \\(\\triangle ABC\\) with vertices \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\).\n\n2. **Draw Altitudes**: \n   Draw the altitudes from each vertex of the triangle, intersecting the opposite side at points \\(D\\), \\(E\\), and \\(F\\) respectively. This creates three perpendicular lines:\n   - \\(AD\\), the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(BC\\)\n   - \\(BE\\), the altitude from \\(B\\) to \\(AC\\)\n   - \\(CF\\), the altitude from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\)\n\n3. **Identify Orthocenter**: \n   Identify the orthocenter of the triangle \\(H\\), which is the point where the three altitudes intersect.\n\n4. **Determine Cyclicity**:\n   According to the properties of triangles, any circle that passes through \\(A, B, C\\) and one point on one of the triangle's altitudes forms a cyclic quadrilateral:\n   \n   - Circle through \\(A\\), \\(D\\), \\(H\\), \\(B\\)\n   - Circle through \\(B\\), \\(E\\), \\(H\\), \\(C\\)\n   - Circle through \\(C\\), \\(F\\), \\(H\\), \\(A\\)\n   \n   From the above, we have three sets of circles, each set represented by the 4 points:\n   - \\(\\{A, D, H, B\\}\\)\n   - \\(\\{B, E, H, C\\}\\)\n   - \\(\\{C, F, H, A\\}\\)\n\n   In total, these form 3 circles.\n\n5. **Point \\(P\\) outside \\(\\triangle ABC\\)**: \n   Place a point \\(P\\) outside of the triangle \\(\\triangle ABC\\). Consequently, point \\(P\\) will not lie on any of the circles already identified. Nor will \\(P\\) lie on any side or altitude of the triangle.\n\n6. **Inversion with respect to \\(P\\)**:\n   Perform an inversion with respect to point \\(P\\) and a specific radius \\(r\\). This is represented as mapping points \\(A, B, C, D, E, F, H\\) to their respective images under inversion:\n   \\[\n   A' = \\text{inverse of } A, \\quad B' = \\text{inverse of } B, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad H' = \\text{inverse of } H\n   \\]\n   This gives us a new set of seven points: \\(A'\\), \\(B'\\), \\(C'\\), \\(D'\\), \\(E'\\), \\(F'\\), \\(H'\\).\n\n7. **Cyclic Quadrilaterals post-inversion**:\n   Given the properties of inversion, any circle that passed through four points prior to inversion will still form a circle passing through the corresponding four inverted points. Thus, our original 6 sets of 4-point cyclic quadrilaterals will yield another 6 sets of 4-point cyclic quadrilaterals:\n   \nThus for the 8 points \\(P, A', B', C', D', E', F', H'\\), we identified at least:\n\\[ 6 (original circles) + 6 (inverted point circles) = 12 \\text{ effective circles} \\]\n\n**Conclusion**:\nHence, we have shown it is possible to arrange 8 points on a plane such that at least 12 \"effective circles\" can be formed. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the number of numbers \\( N \\) from the set \\(\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 2018\\}\\) for which positive solutions \\( x \\) exist for the equation\n\n$$\nx^{[x]} = N\n$$\n\n(where \\([x]\\) is the integer part of the real number \\( x \\), i.e., the largest integer not exceeding \\( x \\)).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem is to find how many numbers N in the set {1, 2, ..., 2018} have at least one positive real solution x for the equation x^[x] = N, where [x] is the integer part of x. Hmm, interesting. I need to figure out for each N in that range, whether there's an x such that when you take x to the power of its floor, you get N. Then count all such N. \n\nFirst, let's make sure I understand the notation. [x] is the floor function, right? So if x is, say, 2.5, then [x] is 2. And the equation becomes x^2 = N. So for each x, depending on its integer part, we have an equation. The challenge is to find for which