 sure they are distinct, compute their approximate numerical values:\n\nFirst solution x \u2248 (1 + 4.123)/2 \u2248 2.5615, y \u2248 (7 + 4.123)/2 \u2248 5.5615\n\nSecond solution x \u2248 (5 + 4.123)/2 \u2248 4.5615, y \u2248 (3 + 4.123)/2 \u2248 3.5615\n\nYes, they are different pairs.\n\nTherefore, the system has two solutions.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe solutions are \\(\\boxed{\\left( \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\frac{7 + \\sqrt{17}}{2} \\right)}\\) and \\(\\boxed{\\left( \\frac{5 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{17}}{2} \\right)}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\n\\log _{x}(y+1) & =4 \\log _{x+2} \\sqrt{y-1} \\\\\n\\log _{y-1}(x+2) & =\\log _{x}\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{y+1}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe start by analyzing the domain restrictions. The bases of the logarithms must be positive and not equal to 1, and the arguments must be positive. This gives us the conditions: \\(x > 0\\), \\(x \\neq 1\\), \\(y > 1\\), and \\(y \\neq 2\\).\n\n### First Equation Transformation\nThe first equation is:\n$$\n\\log_{x}(y + 1) = 4 \\log_{x+2} \\sqrt{y - 1}\n$$\nWe simplify the right-hand side using properties of logarithms:\n$$\n4 \\log_{x+2} \\sqrt{y - 1} = 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\log_{x+2} (y - 1) = 2 \\log_{x+2} (y - 1)\n$$\nConverting both sides to natural logarithms:\n$$\n\\frac{\\ln(y + 1)}{\\ln(x)} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(y - 1)}{\\ln(x + 2)}\n$$\n\n### Second Equation Transformation\nThe second equation is:\n$$\n\\log_{y-1}(x + 2) = \\log_{x}\\left(\\frac{x^3}{y + 1}\\right)\n$$\nConverting both sides to natural logarithms:\n$$\n\\frac{\\ln(x + 2)}{\\ln(y - 1)} = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{x^3}{y + 1}\\right)}{\\ln(x)} = \\frac{3 \\ln x - \\ln(y + 1)}{\\ln x} = 3 - \\frac{\\ln(y + 1)}{\\ln x}\n$$\n\n### Introducing Variables\nLet \\(A = \\frac{\\ln(y + 1)}{\\ln(x)}\\) and \\(B = \\frac{\\ln(y - 1)}{\\ln(x + 2)}\\). The first equation becomes \\(A = 2B\\). The second equation becomes:\n$$\n\\f