d m^{n-1}\n\nBut m divides n!, so m^{n-1} divides n! ?\n\nBut since m divides n!, the multiplicity of each prime factor p in m is at most its multiplicity in n!.\n\nIf m^{n-1} divides n!, then for each prime p dividing m, the multiplicity of p in n! must be at least (n-1)*multiplicity of p in m.\n\nBut since m divides n!, the multiplicity of p in m is at most its multiplicity in n!.\n\nTherefore, (n-1)*mult_p(m) <= mult_p(n!)\n\nBut mult_p(n!) = floor(n/p) + floor(n/p^2) + ...\n\nWhich is <=n/p +n/p^2 + ... <n/(p-1).\n\nTherefore, (n-1)*mult_p(m) <n/(p-1)\n\nBut since mult_p(m) <=mult_p(n!) <n/(p-1)\n\nTherefore, (n-1)*mult_p(m) <n/(p-1) * (n-1)\n\nBut this is not necessarily helpful.\n\nThis seems too abstract. Let's take a concrete example. Suppose n=4, m=2. Then n! =24. m=2 divides 24.\n\nCheck if m^{n-1}=2^3=8 divides 24. 24 /8=3, which is integer. So yes, 8 divides 24. However, for n=4, the equation has no solution, as we saw earlier.\n\nBut suppose m=6 divides 24 (n=4). Then m^{n-1}=6^3=216. 24 /6=4, so t=4. Then check if 4 \u22610 mod 6^{3-1}=36? No. So the condition fails.\n\nBut this is a bit hand-wavy. Perhaps this approach isn't the right way.\n\nLet\u2019s try another angle. Assume that x is an integer root, so x=-m where m divides n!.\n\nThen the equation is:\n\nSum_{k=0}^n (-m)^k /k! =0\n\nMultiply both sides by n!:\n\nSum_{k=0}^n (-m)^k *n! /k! =0\n\nLet\u2019s denote this sum as S=0.\n\nNote that each term for k=0 to n is an integer. Let\u2019s write down the sum:\n\nS =n! -n! m +n! m^2 /2! -n! m^3 /3! + ... + (-1)^n m^n\n\nWe can factor out m from all terms except the first:\n\nS =n! -m [n! -n! m /2! +n! m^2 /3! - ... + (-1)^{n-1} m^{n-1} /n! ]\n\nLet\u2019s denote the expression inside the brackets as T:\n\nT =n! -n! m /2! +n! m^2 /3! - ... + (-1)^{n-1} m^{n-1} /n!\n\nThen S =n! -m T =0 => n! =m T\n\nTherefore, m divides n!.\n\nBut we already knew that. So T =n! /m.\n\nBut T is also an integer, since S and m are integers, and n! -m T =0 => T =n! /m