using the property of the regular tetrahedron (since it's vertex-centric), other edges must also follow the orthogonality.\n\n4. **Determine the Pattern from Geometry**:\n    - Connect midpoints of corresponding opposite edges on faces that share orthogonal relationships. For example:\n        - Consider the midpoint \\( M \\) of edge \\( BC \\). Construct \\( B_1M \\) which projects \\( AB_1 \\).\n        - Given \\( AB_1 \\perp BC_1 \\), thus \\( B_1M \\perp BC_1 \\).\n\n5. **Applying the Perpendicularity Theorem**:\n    - We use the theorem stating if one set of opposite edges in a regular tetrahedron are perpendicular, then cyclic permutations of these vertices result in other sets also being perpendicular.\n\n6. **Count the Number of Remaining Orthogonals**:\n    - By executing similar geometric constructions and permutations for edges \\( B_1C_1, C_1B, B_1C, etc.\\),\n    - We find that each permutation preserves the orthogonal relations.\n\nGiven the orthogonal relationship of \\( AB_1 \\perp BC_1 \\), there remains:\n\n\\[ AB_1 \\perp AA_1, AB_1 \\perp A_1C, BC_1 \\perp CA_1, \\text{ and similar reflections due to symmetry.} \\]\n\nConclusively, this pattern extends to \n- 5 other pairs:\n  - \\( AB_1 \\perp A_1C, A_1B \\perp B_1C, A_1B \\perp AC_1, B_1C \\perp AC_1 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Drawing 2 circles on paper can result in at most 2 intersection points. Drawing 3 circles can result in at most 6 intersection points. Therefore, drawing 10 circles on paper can result in at most how many intersection points?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. So, the question is about drawing 10 circles on paper and finding the maximum number of intersection points possible. They gave examples for 2 and 3 circles, resulting in 2 and 6 intersection points respectively. Hmm, I need to see the pattern here and generalize