ides are in equilibrium, so the pressures are equal. Let's write the initial pressures. For the left side: P_initial_left = (n\u2081 R T) / V\u2081, and for the right side: P_initial_right = (n\u2082 R T) / V\u2082. Since the partition is in equilibrium, these pressures are equal. On the left, n\u2081 is 1 mole of O\u2082. On the right, n\u2082 is 1 mole He + 1 mole O\u2082 = 2 moles total. Therefore:\n\n(1 * R T) / V\u2081 = (2 * R T) / V\u2082\n\nSince R and T are the same on both sides, they cancel out. So:\n\n1 / V\u2081 = 2 / V\u2082 \u2192 V\u2082 = 2 V\u2081\n\nBut since V\u2081 + V\u2082 = V_total = A l (assuming cross-sectional area A), substituting V\u2082 = 2 V\u2081 gives:\n\nV\u2081 + 2 V\u2081 = 3 V\u2081 = A l \u2192 V\u2081 = (A l)/3, and V\u2082 = (2 A l)/3\n\nTherefore, the initial positions of the partition divide the cylinder into 1/3 and 2/3 of the total length. So the initial position is at l/3 from the left end, assuming the left side is the one with 1 mole O\u2082.\n\nNow, at a certain moment, the partition becomes permeable to helium but not to oxygen. So helium can pass through the partition, but oxygen cannot. Since the temperatures remain constant, we need to find the new equilibrium position of the partition. \n\nIn the new equilibrium, the helium can move freely between the two sides, but the oxygen is still confined to their original sides. Wait, but the problem states that the partition is impermeable to oxygen. So initially, the left side has 1 mole O\u2082, and the right side has 1 mole He and 1 mole O\u2082. After the partition becomes permeable to He, helium can move through, but oxygen cannot. So the oxygen remains on their original sides. The helium, however, can redistribute between both sides. \n\nSince the partition is movable, the pressures must still equilibrate, but now the helium can move, so the pressure on each side will be the sum of the partial pressures of oxygen and helium (if present). However, on the left side, in