drilateral, the diagonals intersect at O, so we know that AO + CO = AC and BO + DO = BD. However, unless the diagonals bisect each other, which is the case in a parallelogram, we can't say that AO = CO or BO = DO. But in a rhombus, which is a type of parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other and are perpendicular. So, if ABCD is a rhombus, then AO = CO and BO = DO, and diagonals are perpendicular. However, the problem doesn't state that the diagonals bisect each other; we need to derive that from the given perimeter conditions.\n\nSo, perhaps from these equations, we can deduce relationships between the sides and the diagonal segments.\n\nLet me note that in equations A to D, the sides AB, BC, CD, DA are the sides of the quadrilateral, and AO, BO, CO, DO are the segments of the diagonals.\n\nLet me try to express each equation in terms of adjacent variables.\n\nFrom equation A: AB + AO = BC + CO\n\nFrom equation B: BC + BO = CD + DO\n\nFrom equation C: CD + CO = DA + AO\n\nFrom equation D: DA + DO = AB + BO\n\nNow, if I can express these equations in terms of each other, maybe we can find a relationship between the sides.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can subtract equations to eliminate variables. Let's see.\n\nFrom equations A and C:\n\nA: AB + AO = BC + CO\n\nC: CD + CO = DA + AO\n\nIf we subtract equation C from equation A:\n\nAB + AO - (CD + CO) = BC + CO - (DA + AO)\n\nSimplify left side: AB + AO - CD - CO\n\nRight side: BC + CO - DA - AO\n\nRearranging terms:\n\nAB - CD + AO - CO = BC - DA + CO - AO\n\nBring similar terms to one side:\n\nAB - CD - BC + DA = - (AO - CO) + (CO - AO)\n\nWait, this might be getting too convoluted. Maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider adding all four equations A, B, C, D together.\n\nAdding A: AB + AO = BC + CO\n\nB: BC + BO = CD + DO\n\nC: CD + CO = DA + AO\n\nD: DA + DO = AB + BO\n\nSumming the left sides: AB + AO + BC + BO + CD + CO + DA + DO\n\nSumming the right sides: BC + CO + CD + DO + DA + AO + AB + 