 the sum of the other pair (AB and CD). Hmm. So that's a general property, but not specific enough to conclude a rhombus. For example, a rectangle also has DA + BC = AB + CD, since opposite sides are equal. So in a rectangle, DA = BC and AB = CD, so DA + BC = 2DA and AB + CD = 2AB, which would only be equal if DA = AB, i.e., if it's a square. But in a general rectangle, this is not the case. Wait, no, in a rectangle, DA = BC and AB = CD, so DA + BC = 2DA and AB + CD = 2AB. Therefore, unless DA = AB, these sums are different. So in a rectangle, unless it's a square, DA + BC \u2260 AB + CD. So the equation DA + BC = AB + CD is not generally true for all quadrilaterals, but in our problem, this is a derived condition.\n\nSo, given that DA + BC = AB + CD, this is a specific condition on the quadrilateral. However, we need more conditions to conclude that all sides are equal.\n\nPerhaps we can find another equation. Let's try substituting variables.\n\nFrom equation A: AB + AO = BC + CO. Let's call this equation (1)\n\nFrom equation C: CD + CO = DA + AO. Let's call this equation (3)\n\nFrom equation (1): AO = BC + CO - AB\n\nFrom equation (3): CO = DA + AO - CD\n\nSubstitute AO from equation (1) into equation (3):\n\nCO = DA + (BC + CO - AB) - CD\n\nSimplify:\n\nCO = DA + BC + CO - AB - CD\n\nSubtract CO from both sides:\n\n0 = DA + BC - AB - CD\n\nWhich is the same as before: DA + BC = AB + CD.\n\nSo, this doesn't give new information.\n\nSimilarly, let's try equations B and D.\n\nFrom equation B: BC + BO = CD + DO. Equation (2)\n\nFrom equation D: DA + DO = AB + BO. Equation (4)\n\nFrom equation (4): DO = AB + BO - DA\n\nSubstitute into equation (2):\n\nBC + BO = CD + (AB + BO - DA)\n\nSimplify:\n\nBC + BO = CD + AB + BO - DA\n\nSubtract BO:\n\nBC = CD + AB - DA\n\nWhich is the same as BC + DA = AB + CD. Again, same equation.\n\nSo, no new information here. Thus, the key equation we have is DA + BC = AB + CD. But we need more.\n\nPerhaps we need to consider the other equations. Let's look at equations (1) and (3) again.\n\nFrom equation (1): AB + AO = BC + CO\n\nFrom equation (3): CD + CO = DA + AO\n\nLet me rearrange equation (1):\n\nAB - BC = CO - AO\n\nSimilarly, rearrange equation (3):\n\nCD - DA = AO - CO\n\nNotice that the right-hand sides are negatives of each other:\n\nCO - AO = -(AO - CO)\n\nTherefore, from equations (1) and (3):\n\nAB - BC = -(CD - DA)\n\nWhich gives:\n\nAB - BC = DA - CD\n\nOr,\n\nAB + CD = BC + DA\n\nWait, but we already have from before that DA + BC = AB + CD, so combining these:\n\nIf AB + CD = BC + DA and DA + BC = AB + CD, then these are the same equations. So, no new information here.\n\nHmm. Maybe I need to find expressions for the diagonal segments in terms of the sides.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assign variables to the diagonal segments.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet\u2019s let AO = x, BO = y, CO = z, DO = w.\n\nSo, the diagonals are AC = x + z and BD = y + w.\n\nThen, the perimeters:\n\n1. AB + y + x = p\n\n2. BC + z + y = p\n\n3. CD + w + z = p\n\n4. DA + x + w = p\n\nSo, equations:\n\n1. AB + x + y = p\n\n2. BC + y + z = p\n\n3. CD + z + w = p\n\n4. DA + w + x = p\n\nWe can write these as:\n\nAB = p - x - y\n\nBC = p - y - z\n\nCD = p - z - w\n\nDA = p - w - x\n\nNow, since ABCD is a quadrilateral, the sum of its sides is AB + BC + CD + DA.\n\nLet\u2019s compute that:\n\nAB + BC + CD + DA = (p - x - y) + (p - y - z) + (p - z - w) + (p - w - x)\n\n= 4p - 2x - 2y - 2z - 2w\n\nBut we also know that the sum of the diagonals is AC + BD = (x + z) + (y + w) = x + y + z + w\n\nTherefore, AB + BC + CD + DA = 4p - 2(x + y + z + w) = 4p - 2(AC + BD)\n\nBut I don't know if that's helpful yet.\n\nAlternatively, since we have expressions for AB, BC, CD, DA in terms of x, y, z, w, maybe we can find relations between them.\n\nFrom the expressions:\n\nAB = p - x - y\n\nBC = p - y - z\n\nCD = p - z - w\n\nDA = p - w - x\n\nIf we can express all sides in terms of x, y, z, w, perhaps we can find that AB = BC = CD = D