l vertices.\n   - Using coordinates and distance equations, we find the center \\(C\\) divides \\(SO\\) such that:\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{R^2 + k^2} = h - k\n     \\]\n   - Solving for \\(k\\) (distance from base to center \\(C\\)):\n     \\[\n     k = \\frac{h^2 - R^2}{2h}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(R = h \\tan \\alpha\\), we get:\n     \\[\n     k = \\frac{h(1 - \\tan^2 \\alpha)}{2}\n     \\]\n   - The distance from \\(C\\) to the apex \\(S\\) is:\n     \\[\n     h - k = \\frac{h(1 + \\tan^2 \\alpha)}{2}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Final Ratio**:\n   - The ratio \\(CO : CS\\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1 - \\tan^2 \\alpha}{1 + \\tan^2 \\alpha}\n     \\]\n   - Using trigonometric identities, this simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     \\cos 2\\alpha : 1\n     \\]\n\nThus, the center of the circumscribed sphere divides the height of the pyramid in the ratio \\(\\boxed{\\cos 2\\alpha : 1}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the regular triangular pyramid \\(SABC\\) with height \\(SA\\) and orthocenter \\(O\\). The height \\(SA\\) perpendicular to the base \\(ABC\\) meets at the centroid of the base. Denote the side length of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\) as \\(x\\).\n   \n2. Since the problem gives us the angle \\(\\alpha\\) between the height \\(SA\\) of the pyramid and a lateral edge \\(SO\\), let's form triangle \\(SOA\\) (Figure 12.94). Here, \\(SO = OA\\) by the properties of equilateral triangles.\n\n3. From the properties of triangle \\(\\triangle SOA\\), we observe that \\(OD \\perp SA\\) and divides \\(SA\\) into \\(SD\\) and \\(DA\\). Since \\(SD = DA = \\frac{x}{2}\\) (equilateral triangle properties):\n\n   \\[\n   SD = \\frac{x}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Consider triangle \\(\\triangle SDO\\), where:\n\n   \\[\n   SD = SO \\cos \\alpha\n   \\]\n   \n   \\(x = 2 SO \\cos \\alpha\\) implies:\n\n   \\[\n   SO = \\frac{x}{2 \\cos \\alpha}\n   \\]\n\n5. Now, considering triangle \\(\\triangle SO_1A\\), where \\(O_1\\) is the center of the circumscribed sphere. Here 