ss $c_n$:\n   \\[\n   c_n = a_n + b_n = 2^n + 3^n.\n   \\]\n\n2. Compute the first few terms of $\\left\\{c_{n} \\right\\}$:\n   \\[\n   c_1 = 2^1 + 3^1 = 5,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_2 = 2^2 + 3^2 = 4 + 9 = 13,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_3 = 2^3 + 3^3 = 8 + 27 = 35,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_4 = 2^4 + 3^4 = 16 + 81 = 97.\n   \\]\n\n3. Use \\( \\left\\{c_{n+1} - p c_{n}\\right\\} \\) to form the next terms which should be a geometric sequence:\n   \\[\n   c_{2} - p c_{1} = 13 - 5p,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_{3} - p c_{2} = 35 - 13p,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_{4} - p c_{3} = 97 - 35p.\n   \\]\n\n4. Since $\\left\\{c_{n+1} - p c_{n}\\right\\}$ is a geometric sequence, the ratio between any consecutive terms is constant:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c_{3} - p c_{2}}{c_{2} - p c_{1}} = \\frac{c_{4} - p c_{3}}{c_{3} - p c_{2}}.\n   \\]\n\n5. Plug in the expressions from step 3:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{35 - 13p}{13 - 5p} = \\frac{97 - 35p}{35 - 13p}.\n   \\]\n\n6. Cross multiply to solve for $p$:\n   \\[\n   (35 - 13p)(35 - 13p) = (13 - 5p)(97 - 35p),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (35 - 13p)^2 = (13 - 5p)(97 - 35p).\n   \\]\n\n7. Expand both sides:\n   \\[\n   1225 - 910p + 169p^2 = 1261 - 596p - 175p + 25p^2,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1225 - 910p + 169p^2 = 1261 - 771p + 25p^2.\n   \\]\n\n8. Combine like terms and simplify:\n   \\[\n   169p^2 - 910p + 1225 = 25p^2 - 771p + 1261,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   144p^2 - 139p - 36 = 0.\n   \\]\n\n9. Factoring or using the quadratic formula gives us:\n   \\[\n   p = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad p = 3.\n   \\]\n\n10. Verify that both roots work:\n   - If $p=2$, the sequence $\\left\\{c_{n+1}-2c_{n}\\right\\}$ forms a geometric sequence.\n   - If $p=3$, the sequence $\\left\\{c_{n+1}-3c_{n}\\right\\}$ also forms a geometric sequence.\n\nSo, the value of $p$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{2, 3\\}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n to Part (2):\n\nTo show that the sequence $ \\left\\{c_{n}\\right\\}$ isn't a geometric sequence if the common ratios of $\\left\\{a_{n}\\right\\}$ and $\\left\\{b_{n}\\right\\}$ are not equal:\n\n1. Assume $\\left\\{a_{n}\\right\\}$ has a common ratio $q_{1}$ and $\\left\\{b_{n}\\right\\}$ has a common ratio $q_{2}$, with $q_{1} \\neq q_{2}$.\n   \\[\n   a_{n} = a q_{1}^{n-1}, \\quad b_{n} = b q_{2}^{n-1}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Then:\n   \\[\n   c_{n} = a_{n} + b_{n} = a q_{1}^{n-1} + b q_{2}^{n-1}.\n   \\]\n\n3. For a sequence to be geometric, the squared term of the middle term must equal the product of its neighbors:\n   \\[\n   c_{1} \\cdot c_{3} = (a + b)(a q_{1}^{2} + b q_{2}^{2}),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   c_{2}^{2} = (a q_{1} + b q_{2})^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n4. Evaluate the difference:\n   \\[\n   c_{1} \\cdot c_{3} - c_{2}^{2} = (a + b)(a q_{1}^{2} + b q_{2}^{2}) - (a q_{1} + b q_{2})^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify this expression:\n   \\[\n   (a + b)(a q_{1}^{2} + b q_{2}^{2}) - (a q_{1} + b q_{2})^{2} = a^2 q_{1}^2 + ab q_{2}^2 + ab q_{1}^2 + b^2 q_{2}^2 - (a^2 q_{1}^2 + 2ab q_{1} q_{2} + b^2 q_{2}^2),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = ab(q_{2}^2 - q_{1} q_{2} q_{1} + q_{1}^2 - q_{1} q_{2} q_{2}) \\neq 0 \\quad \\text{since} \\quad q_{1} \\neq q_{2}.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the sequence $\\left\\{c_{n}\\right\\}$ is not geometric:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{c_{n}\\right\\} \\text{ is not a geometric sequence.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( n \\) be the minimum number of circles with radius 1 required to completely cover a given polygon \\( M \\). Let \\( m \\) be the maximum number of disjoint circles with radius 1 whose centers are inside the polygon \\( M \\). Which is larger, \\( n \\) or \\( m \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out whether the minimum number of circles (radius 1) required to cover a polygon M, which is n, is larger than the maximum number of disjoint circles (radius 1) with centers inside M, which is m, or vice versa. The question is asking which is larger, n or m. Hmm, interesting problem. Let me start by understanding the concepts involved here.\n\nFirst, cove