\( m \\geq 11 \\) initially and decreases by at least 1 each step, it must eventually reach values below 11.\n   - Once \\( m \\leq 10 \\), further subtractions reduce \\( m \\) by 1 until reaching \\( m = 7 \\), corresponding to \\( n = 63 \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: Regardless of the starting three-digit number, the process reduces \\( m \\) until it reaches 7, ensuring \\( 63 \\) appears. Hence, 63 is guaranteed in the sequence.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{63}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Problem**:\n    - Let \\( A \\) be an integer such that \\( 100 < A < 1000 \\).\n    - Repeatedly subtract the sum of its digits from \\( A \\) until a smaller number is reached.\n\n2. **Properties of Digits and Modulo 9**:\n    - It is known that a number has the same remainder when divided by 9 as the sum of its digits.\n    - Let's denote the sum of the digits of \\( A \\) as \\( S(A) \\).\n    - Mathematically, we can write: \\( A \\equiv S(A) \\pmod{9} \\).\n\n3. **First Step Calculation**:\n    - In the first step, subtracting the sum of the digits from \\( A \\):\n    \\[\n    A - S(A)\n    \\]\n    - The result of this subtraction will be divisible by 9 because:\n    \\[\n    A \\equiv S(A) \\pmod{9} \\implies A - S(A) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}\n    \\]\n    - Therefore, \\( A - S(A) \\) is divisible by 9.\n\n4. **Subsequent Steps**:\n    - Each subsequent result, being the difference of a 9-divisible number and the sum of its digits, remains divisible by 9.\n\n5. **Numbers Less Than 100**:\n    - Consider the multiples of 9 that are less than 100. These are:\n    \\[\n    9, 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90, 99\n    \\]\n    - Noting that, except for 99, all other multiples of 9 below 100 have a digit sum of either 9 or 18.\n\n6. **Sequence Convergence**:\n    - Since each step reduces the number by at least 9, within a few steps we will reach a number less than 100. The digit sum of a three-digit number cannot exceed 27, further ensuring convergence.\n\n7. **Minimum Value Check**:\n    - The minimum number greater than 63 which, when summed to its digits gets above 100, is 63 plus a multiple of 9:\n    \\[\n    63 + 36 = 99\n    \\]\n    - Hence, numbers will converge below 100 quickly. If at any point 99 is reached:\n    \\[\n    99 - 9 = 90 \\rightarrow 81 \\rightarrow 72 \\rightarrow 63\n    \\]\n    - If the value is below 99 but larger than 63, the decrement by 9 ensures we reach 63 in a few steps.\n\n8. **Final Result**:\n    - Therefore, the process inevitably leads to the number 63.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{63}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Every 4 years, the population of the town of Arloe doubles. On December 31, 2008, the population of Arloe was 3456. What was the population on December 31, 1988? \n(A) 54 \n(B) 576 \n(C) 216 \n(D) 108 \n(E) 864", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this population problem. So, the question is: Every 4 years, the population of Arloe doubles. On December 31, 2008, the population was 3456. What was the population on December 31, 1988? The options are given as (A) 54, (B) 576, (C) 216, (D) 108, (E) 864.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the time difference between 1988 and 2008. Let me subtract 1988 from 2008. That gives 20 years. Since the population doubles every 4 years, I need to figure out how many 4-year periods are in 20 years. Dividing 20 by 4 gives 5. So, there are 5 periods of 4 years each between 1988 and 2008.\n\nBut wait, the population doubles every 4 years, so if we go back in time, each 4-year period would mean dividing the population by 2. Since we're starting from 2008 and going back to 1988, that's 20 years back, which is 5 doubling periods. Therefore, the population in 1988 would be 3456 divided by 2 five times.\n\nLet me write that down step by step to make sure I don't make a mistake.\n\nStarting population in 2008: 3456\n\nGoing back 4 years to 2004: 3456 / 