1*4)=0.82075. Angle\u224834.7 degrees. Convex.\n\nAt point D(3.283,-2.283): angle between DC and DA. DC=(-2.283,3.283), DA=(-3.283,2.283). The dot product=(-2.283)(-3.283)+(3.283)(2.283)\u22487.5 +7.5\u224815. Magnitudes: DC\u22484, DA\u22484. Cosine of angle\u224815/(4*4)=15/16\u22480.9375. Angle\u224820 degrees. Convex.\n\nAt point A(0,0): angle between AD and AB. AD=(3.283,-2.283), AB=(1,0). Dot product=3.283*1 + (-2.283)*0=3.283. Magnitudes: AD\u22484, AB=1. Cosine of angle\u22483.283/(4*1)=0.82075. Angle\u224834.7 degrees. Convex.\n\nAll angles less than 180, so the quadrilateral is convex. In this case, exactly two sides (AB and BC) are shorter than the longest diagonal BD\u22483.227, and the other two sides (CD and DA=4) are longer. Therefore, the answer to part b) is yes, there can be exactly two such sides.\n\nIn summary:\n\nFor part a), using the theorem that in any convex quadrilateral, the sum of the diagonals is greater than the sum of any pair of opposite sides, we can conclude that at least two sides must be shorter than the longest diagonal.\n\nFor part b), by constructing a convex quadrilateral where exactly two sides are shorter than the longest diagonal, we show that such a case is possible.", "deepseek_solution": "### Final Answer\n\na) \\boxed{\\text{In any convex quadrilateral, at least two sides are shorter than the longest diagonal.}}  \nb) \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}  \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n**Part a) Proof:**  \n1. **Theorem Application:** In any convex quadrilateral, the sum of the lengths of the diagonals is greater than the sum of any pair of opposite sides.  \n2. **Assume Longest Diagonal:** Let the longest diagonal be \\( AC \\).  \n3. **Opposite Sides Consideration:** Consider pairs of opposite sides \\( (AB, CD) \\) and \\( (BC, DA) \\).  \n4. **Contradiction Approach:** If both sides in a pair were \u2265 \\( AC \\), their sum would exceed \\( AC + BD \\) (using the theorem), implying \\( BD > AC \\), contradicting \\( AC \\) 