ed the value of \\(BD\\).\n\nThus, we have shown that there are at least two sides, \\(AB\\) and \\(AD\\), that are shorter than the diagonal \\(BD\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{BD > AB \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BD > AD}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b):\nWe need to determine if it is possible to have exactly two sides shorter than the longest diagonal.\n\nYes, it is possible to have exactly two sides shorter than the longest diagonal in a convex quadrilateral. \n\n**Example:**\n\n1. Consider an isosceles triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with \\( AB = BC \\) and \\(AB > AC\\).\n2. Let \\( M \\) be the midpoint of \\(AC\\), and extend the altitude from \\(B\\) to \\(M\\) such that it passes through \\(M\\) and intersects at a new point \\(D\\) such that \\(MD < AB - BM\\).\n3. Construct quad literals \\((AB, BD, DC, CA)\\) such that \\( \\angle BMC = 90^\\circ \\), then side \\(AD\\) is created with \\((AC)\\) bisect to \\(BD\\) making \\(BCD\\) isosceles.\n\nNow both sides \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) are equal, exceeding \\(BD\\), \\(BM\\) and making four-sided figure convex with segment angles sum back to \\(360^\\circ\\)\n\nOn arranging tri sides with additional angle from \\(D\\) sum becomes \\(180^\\circ - b\\)\\approx spacing extra below the longest diagonal thus apart of angle construct length bounds from the triangle inequality valid. Hence,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{YES}, \\text{ both of such sides } experience the case created under stated conditions.}\n\\]\n\nThus our conclusion reached confirms both dimensional relationship and example maintain feasibility under steps shown above \\(\\blacksquare\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $a$, $b$, and $c$ be real numbers greater than 1. Prove the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{a b}{c-1}+\\frac{b c}{a-1}+\\frac{c a}{b-1} \\geq 12 .\n$$\n\nWhen does equality hold?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove this inequality: (ab)/(c-1) + (bc)/(a-1) + (ca)/(b-1) \u2265 12, whe