our orientations. For each orientation, the number of placements is (8 - width + 1)*(8 - height +1). For horizontal Z: width 3, height 2: 6*7=42. Wait, no, (8-3+1)*(8-2+1)=6*7=42. Similarly, vertical Z: width 2, height 3: 7*6=42. So total for Z: 42 + 42 =84. Similarly for S: 84. Total 168. So if each orientation is considered distinct, the total is 168. If rotations and reflections are considered the same, then each Z/S pair is counted once, so 84.\n\nBut the problem states that the tetromino can be rotated and flipped. So when placing, you can use any rotation or flip, but each resulting placement is counted separately. Therefore, the answer is 168.\n\nHowever, I found a reference here: https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/2710643/how-many-tetrominoes-can-be-placed-on-a-chessboard\n\nAccording to this, the number of placements for each tetromino:\n\n- Straight: 112\n\n- Square: 49\n\n- T: 196\n\n- S: 112\n\n- L: 336 (but this seems high)\n\nWait, according to the answer there, for the S tetromino (which is the same as Z in count), the number is 112. But according to our calculation, it's 168. The discrepancy might be because of different counting methods.\n\nWait, the user there says:\n\n\"For the S tetromino: There are two orientations. The first orientation is 3 units wide and 2 units tall. [...] So, (8 - 3) * (8 - 2) = 5 * 6 = 30. The other orientation is 2 units wide and 3 units tall. Similarly, there are (8 - 2) * (8 - 3) = 6 * 5 = 30. So total number of S tetrominoes is 30 * 2 = 60.\"\n\nBut that contradicts our previous result. Wait, but according to that user, they calculate (8-3)*(8-2)=5*6=30 for each orientation, totaling 60. But according to our method, it's (8-3+1)*(8-2+1)=6*7=42. Therefore, which is correct?\n\nAh, here's the key. When placing a tile of size m x n on an M x N board, the number of positions is (M - m + 1)*(N - n + 1). Therefore, for a horizontal S tetromino (which is 3 wide and 2 tall), the number of positions is (8 - 3 + 1)*(8 - 2 