iables, the symmetry is cyclic rather than full. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form:\n\n\\[\n\\sum \\frac{a_i^2}{b_i} \\geq \\frac{(\\sum a_i)^2}{\\sum b_i}\n\\]\n\nBut here, the numerators aren't squares in the same way. Wait, the numerators are 1 + x\u00b2, which is similar to a squared term. Maybe we can set up Cauchy-Schwarz by considering numerators and denominators appropriately. Let me try that.\n\nSuppose I consider each term as \\(\\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + y + z^2}\\). If I set up vectors for Cauchy-Schwarz, maybe the numerators could be part of the squares in the numerator. Let's try:\n\nLet\u2019s denote for each term, the numerator is (1 + x\u00b2) and denominator is (1 + y + z\u00b2). If I apply Cauchy-Schwarz to the sum, then:\n\n\\[\n\\sum \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + y + z^2} \\geq \\frac{(1 + x + 1 + y + 1 + z)^2}{\\sum (1 + y + z^2)(1 + x^2)}\n\\]\n\nWait, but that seems complicated. Let me check. The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum \\frac{a_i^2}{b_i} \\right) \\geq \\frac{(\\sum a_i)^2}{\\sum b_i}\n\\]\n\nSo if I let a_i = sqrt(1 + x\u00b2), and b_i = 1 + y + z\u00b2, then:\n\nLeft-hand side would be sum (1 + x\u00b2)/(1 + y + z\u00b2), which is our original sum. The right-hand side would be [sum sqrt(1 + x\u00b2)]^2 / [sum (1 + y + z\u00b2)]. Hmm, not sure if this helps. Let's compute the denominator sum:\n\nSum of denominators: [1 + y + z\u00b2] + [1 + z + x\u00b2] + [1 + x + y\u00b2] = 3 + x + y + z + x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2.\n\nAnd the numerator of the RHS in Cauchy-Schwarz would be [sqrt(1 + x\u00b2) + sqrt(1 + y\u00b2) + sqrt(1 + z\u00b2)]\u00b2.\n\nSo the inequality would give:\n\nOur sum >= [sqrt(1 + x\u00b2) + sqrt(1 + y\u00b2) + sqrt(1 + z\u00b2)]\u00b2 / (3 + x + y + z + x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2).\n\nBut we need to show that the original sum is >=2. So if we can show that this fraction is >=2, then we would have the result. But not sure if that's the case. Maybe this ap