\leq \\frac{3}{4} x + \\frac{1}{4} y.\n   \\]\n   Summing up all terms:\n   \\[\n   \\sum x^{3/4} y^{1/4} \\leq \\frac{3}{4}(x + y + z) + \\frac{1}{4}(x + y + z) = 3.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine Results:**\n   Substituting back, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sum \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^3 + b}} \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the inequality is proven, and equality holds when \\( a = b = c = 1 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the condition $\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} = 3$ for positive real numbers $a, b, c$, we need to prove the following inequality:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^3 + b}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{b^3 + c}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c^3 + a}} \\leq \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]\n\nWe will use the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) inequality in our proof. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers, the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean.\n\n1. **Applying AM-GM Inequality:**\n   By the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n   \\[\n   a^3 + b \\geq 2\\sqrt{a^3 \\cdot b}\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{a^3 + b} \\geq \\sqrt{2\\sqrt{a^3 \\cdot b}} = \\sqrt{2a^{3/2} \\sqrt{b}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^3 + b}} \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2a^{3/2} \\sqrt{b}}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplifying the Expression:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^3 + b}} &\\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{a^{3/2}} \\sqrt[4]{b}} \\\\\n   &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2a^{3/2} \\sqrt{b}}}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly for $b^3 + c$ and $c^3 + a$, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{b^3 + c}} \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2b^{3/2} \\sqrt{c}}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c^3 + a}} \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2c^{3/2} \\sqrt{a}}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combining the Inequalities:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^3 + b}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{b^3 + c}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c^3 + a}} &\\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2a^{3/2} \\sqr