 y*(c_y - a_y) = 0.\n\nAlso, since E is on AC, E = A + s*(C - A), so coordinates of E are:\n\nE_x = a_x + s*(c_x - a_x)\n\nE_y = a_y + s*(c_y - a_y)\n\nE_z = a_z + s*(c_z - a_z) = 0.5\n\nSo from E_z = 0.5, s = (0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z)\n\nTherefore, x = a_x + [(0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z)]*(c_x - a_x)\n\ny = a_y + [(0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z)]*(c_y - a_y)\n\nThen vector DE is (x, y, 0) since E_z = 0.5 and D is (0,0,0.5). So DE = (x, y, 0)\n\nTherefore, DE \u2022 (C - A) = 0 gives:\n\nx*(c_x - a_x) + y*(c_y - a_y) = 0\n\nSubstituting x and y:\n\n[a_x + s*(c_x - a_x)]*(c_x - a_x) + [a_y + s*(c_y - a_y)]*(c_y - a_y) = 0\n\nLet me expand this:\n\na_x*(c_x - a_x) + s*(c_x - a_x)^2 + a_y*(c_y - a_y) + s*(c_y - a_y)^2 = 0\n\nFactor out s:\n\ns*[(c_x - a_x)^2 + (c_y - a_y)^2] + a_x*(c_x - a_x) + a_y*(c_y - a_y) = 0\n\nBut s is (0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z). Let's denote s = (0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z)\n\nSo substituting:\n\n[(0.5 - a_z)/(c_z - a_z)]*[(c_x - a_x)^2 + (c_y - a_y)^2] + a_x*(c_x - a_x) + a_y*(c_y - a_y) = 0\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe there is a better way to model this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assuming some coordinates for points A and C to simplify. Since AC is sqrt(6), and given the areas of the faces, perhaps we can find relations between the coordinates.\n\nGiven that the area of face ASB is equal to the area of face BSC, and the area of face ASC is twice that of BSC.\n\nLet me recall that the area of a triangle can be calculated using the cross product. For example, the area of face ASB is (1/2)*|AS \u00d7 AB|, but wait, AS and AB are vectors from point A? Wait, no. The area of triangle ASB is (1/2)*|SB \u00d7 SA|. Since SB and SA are vectors from point S to B and A, respectively.\n\nWait, maybe better to use coordinates. Let's denote vectors SB, SA, SC, etc., and compute their cross products.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express the areas in terms of coordinates.\n\nGiven that S is at (0,0,1), B is at (0,0,0). Let me assign coordinates to A and C.\n\nLet me denote point A 